"""Evidence-based content for the official AIN report template."""
import json
from pathlib import Path

def write_report(b):
    root=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
    stress=json.loads((root/'results/stress/metrics.json').read_text())['scenarios']
    abstract=('Visual Waste Sorting Assistant is an offline educational application that predicts one of six waste categories from a photograph and presents material preparation guidance. The project integrates an existing, from-scratch PyTorch convolutional neural network rather than replacing or retraining it during product development. TrashNet contains 2,527 images, split into 1,766 training, 326 validation and 435 test examples with seed 42. The selected aug_none network has 114,966 trainable parameters, receives 64 by 64 RGB inputs and uses dropout of 0.3. Its original held-out test accuracy is 76.8% and Macro-F1 is 75.7%; a new evaluation with the shared product inference pipeline reproduces 334 correct predictions out of 435. Final-stage work adds validated uploads, six-class confidence visualization, source-linked guidance, a redesigned four-page interface and reproducible stress evaluation. Across 1,740 controlled low-light and blur variants, accuracy falls to 48.6% and Macro-F1 to 48.4%, showing sensitivity to darkness and reduced contrast. A 12-image complex subset supports descriptive inspection only. Real-camera low-light and genuinely new campus photographs remain unavailable. Confidence scores are uncalibrated, unknown objects receive known labels, and high-confidence mistakes prevent correctness guarantees. The product supports an academic demonstration while keeping these evidence gaps explicit.')
    b.front(abstract)
    # MAIN BODY PAGE 1
    b.h('1  Introduction and Problem Definition',1)
    b.h('1.1  Context and Motivation')
    b.p('Students and campus visitors encounter packaging made from paper, cardboard, glass, metal and plastic, as well as mixed residual items. Appearance alone can make these materials difficult to distinguish. A visual assistant can help a user inspect an item and find preparation advice, provided it explains uncertainty and does not present a predicted material as an authoritative collection decision.')
    b.p('We built Visual Waste Sorting Assistant for this task. It accepts one photograph, predicts a material category and presents examples, preparation steps, warnings and source links. The intended setting is a university demonstration or supervised learning activity. We do not claim deployment at Hanoi University, integration with its collection service, or measured reductions in contamination: no such operational study has been conducted.')
    b.h('1.2  Problem Statement')
    b.p('The learning problem is closed-set classification using TrashNet [1]. The taxonomy has six labels in a fixed order. The product adds an interaction layer around the original PyTorch implementation [2]. Table 1 defines the task boundaries and the claims the interface can legitimately make.')
    b.table(['Item','Description'],[['Input','One JPEG or PNG photograph containing a waste item.'],['Output','Cardboard, glass, metal, paper, plastic or trash; six scores and educational guidance.'],['Constraints','Offline inference; original CNN; RGB 64 × 64; uploads ≤10 MiB, ≤20 MP and ≤8192 px per side.'],['Assumptions','An identifiable item fits the taxonomy. Safety and collector acceptance require separate checks.']],'Problem specification.',sample=2)
    # PAGE 2
    b.page();b.h('1.3  Objectives and Contributions')
    b.p('Our first contribution is a reproducible connection between the trained AI core and the final application. The application and evaluator load the same selected checkpoint, preserve class order and use the same deterministic model preprocessing. A SHA-256 checksum identifies the checkpoint, its architecture loads strictly, and its output is checked for six finite logits. This prevents an interface from silently using a different network or inconsistent labels.')
    b.p('Our second contribution is a usable image-to-guidance workflow. Users inspect an image before prediction, see all six confidence scores, and read preparation and safety guidance. Upload handling covers grayscale, transparency, image integrity, EXIF orientation and dimension limits. The redesigned interface separates classification, model information, failure modes and robustness results. It reports input and model errors rather than substituting invented predictions.')
    b.p('Our third contribution is a manifest-driven evaluation framework for four visual conditions. Standard images, a complex descriptive subset, synthetic low-light/blur variants, real-camera captures and campus photographs have distinct provenance labels. Synthetic and real-camera results are separated within the low-light condition. Outputs include per-image scores, confusion matrices, metrics, example galleries, timing and source hashes.')
    b.p('Our fourth contribution is an explicit account of the evidence. The selected CNN reproduces its existing benchmark, but perturbations expose a substantial loss of performance. The complex subset overlaps the standard set; real-camera and campus evaluations remain pending. These qualifications are central findings. A successful software demonstration is not equivalent to independent field validation.')
    b.p('The objective is broader than increasing a classification score. It is to deliver an executable product whose behavior can be inspected and defended. We retain the midterm AI core as the reference, preserve unsuccessful ablations, and distinguish software improvements from unmeasured gains in generalization. No retraining or final-challenge tuning was performed during the product upgrade. Material recognition and collection advice remain separate components so that changing an educational instruction cannot alter a model prediction.')
    # PAGE 3
    b.page();b.h('2  AI Background and Formal Formulation',1);b.h('2.1  Formal Problem Formulation')
    b.p('Let D be N labeled images (xᵢ,yᵢ). Each input xᵢ is a three-channel 64 by 64 tensor in [0,1], and yᵢ is an integer from 0 to 5. The hypothesis fθ is the CNN with parameters θ. It maps an image to six real logits z. Equation (1) gives the mapping and decision rule; ties follow the first maximum in the fixed class order.')
    b.equation('formulation')
    b.p('A logit is an unconstrained score, not a probability. For display, softmax converts the six logits as in Equation (2). Subtracting the maximum a improves numerical stability without changing ratios or the predicted class. The scores sum to one by normalization; this identity does not establish calibration or validate an unknown-object decision.')
    b.equation('softmax')
    b.p('Training minimizes weighted cross-entropy. For class k with nₖ training examples, its weight is Ntrain/(6nₖ). Let B be a mini-batch, w the target-class weights and sᵢ,yᵢ the score of the correct class. PyTorch uses the weighted mean in Equation (3), dividing by the sum of target weights rather than batch size.')
    b.equation('loss')
    b.p('Weights depend on training labels only. Validation selects the checkpoint epoch and eligible configuration; the final test split does not guide selection. CrossEntropyLoss receives logits directly. Applying softmax before that loss would change the objective. At inference, gradients are disabled and evaluation mode ensures that dropout and Batch Normalization use their intended inference behavior.')
    # PAGE 4
    b.page();b.h('2.2  Selected Method')
    b.p('Shared kernels extract local features. PyTorch Conv2d implements cross-correlation [3]. For input channel c, output channel o, kernel offsets u,v, stride s and padding p, Equation (4) sums weighted input values. Values outside the image are zero. The selected convolutions have no biases; u,v span a 3 by 3 kernel, and c spans input channels.')
    b.equation('conv');b.equation('dimensions')
    b.p('Equation (5) gives the output height for unit dilation; width follows the same rule. A 3 by 3 kernel, stride one and padding one preserves size. For a hand-worked example, apply an all-ones 2 by 2 kernel to an image with rows (1,2,3), (4,5,6), (7,8,9). Stride one and no padding produce ((12,16),(24,28)); floor((3−2)/1)+1 = 2. A 2 by 2 max pool then returns 28.')
    b.p('Each block uses Conv, Batch Normalization, ReLU and stride-two max pooling. ReLU returns max(0,z). Batch Normalization learns scale γ and offset β after normalization [4], as in Equation (6). Inference uses checkpoint running statistics, not statistics estimated from the uploaded photograph.')
    b.equation('bn')
    b.p('Global average pooling averages each final feature map, yielding 128 features without trainable parameters. Inverted dropout uses Bernoulli masks and rescales survivors during training [5], as in Equation (7); inference uses the identity. Two dropout layers surround the dense ReLU head. The final dense layer outputs six logits.')
    b.equation('dropout')
    # PAGE 5
    b.page();b.h('2.3  Baseline and Related Work')
    b.p('Two simple baselines establish reference performance. The majority-class baseline always predicts the most frequent training label. The color-histogram centroid baseline represents RGB images with histograms and compares an image with class centroids estimated from training data. Both use the same split and six-class scoring as the CNN. They are useful controls, although they do not isolate every difference in feature capacity or optimization.')
    b.p('The selected network is implemented from scratch without pretrained features. Four blocks use channels 3→16→32→64→128 and spatial sizes 64→32→16→8→4. Adaptive average pooling is followed by dropout 0.3, Linear(128,128), ReLU, dropout 0.3 and Linear(128,6). Convolution weights total 97,200. Batch Normalization contributes 480, the first dense layer 16,512 and the final layer 774, totaling 114,966 trainable parameters. Running statistics are buffers and are excluded.')
    b.p('Batch Normalization and dropout affect training through different mechanisms [4], [5]; neither guarantees generalization on campus. Calibration research explains why a normalized score cannot automatically be interpreted as observed correctness likelihood [6]. We present scores as uncalibrated confidence, retain high-score mistakes and do not implement an unsupported unknown-object detector. Fitting calibration on the final challenge images would compromise their independence.')
    b.p('An exploratory comparison used Qwen2.5-VL 3B through Ollama on 30 images: the CNN achieved 25/30 and Qwen 20/30. Original approximate timings were 0.01 and 1.28 seconds per image. These are historical project observations, not a new controlled comparison here. Sample size, prompting, output parsing and hardware prevent general claims that CNNs outperform vision-language models. The final application does not call Qwen, Ollama or an external language-model API. This distinction matters because an offline product must retain its own implemented AI core rather than delegate classification to a hosted service.')
    # PAGE 6
    b.page();b.h('3  System Architecture and Algorithm Design',1);b.h('3.1  User Flow')
    b.p('The user opens Waste Classification Studio and uploads an image. Figure 1 annotates the desktop workspace: A is navigation, B the upload control, C the preview area, and D the prediction workflow. Metadata cards describe categories, input resolution and model identity; they are not live performance claims. The interface uses Streamlit [7].')
    b.image('studio.png',6.15,'Annotated desktop workspace. A navigation; B upload; C preview; D review workflow. Screenshot of the project application.')
    b.p('After upload, the application shows the oriented image, format and dimensions. Predict runs the original CNN and displays material, score and inference time. Class Confidence Analysis shows six bars and highlights the leading category. Guidance follows with preparation, cautions and source links. Predictions persist while reviewing guidance but clear when the image changes, preventing stale output from appearing beside a new photograph.')
    # PAGE 7
    b.page();b.p('Figure 2 shows the application at a 390-pixel viewport. A marks compact metadata and B the upload card. Navigation can expand or collapse, while cards stack vertically. Responsive layout was inspected in a browser; this is a usability check, not evidence of classifier accuracy on phone photographs.')
    b.image('mobile.png',2.35,'Annotated small-screen workspace. A metadata; B upload. Screenshot of the actual responsive application.')
    b.p('The remaining pages support review. Model Information exposes architecture, training configuration and benchmarks. Known Limitations and Failure Modes shows visual confusions and confident mistakes. Robustness Testing displays actual sample counts, metrics and galleries, with pending states for missing camera and campus data. None changes model weights or interprets low confidence as a validated unknown-class decision. The user must check the physical material and local collection arrangements before acting on educational advice.')
    # PAGE 8
    b.page();b.h('3.2  System Overview')
    b.p('Figure 3 separates interface, validation, inference and guidance. Uploaded bytes stay in the local application process for prediction. Pillow [8] verifies JPEG/PNG contents, corrects EXIF orientation, converts grayscale to RGB and composites transparency on white. These upload adaptations are explicit; the historical benchmark retains its original conversion rather than silently changing test images.')
    b.image('system.png',6.15,'High-level architecture. Each box is an implemented local component; guidance data is separate from the model.')
    b.p('The shared inference module resizes RGB images bilinearly to 64 by 64, divides float32 pixels by 255 and arranges channels first, without mean/std normalization. NumPy [9] supports arrays and metrics; Matplotlib [10] generates figures. The cached loader validates checkpoint format, classes, preprocessing metadata and the strict state dictionary. Model loading is reused across predictions.')
    b.h('3.3  Algorithm Design')
    for line in ['Algorithm 1  Validated image classification','Input: JPEG/PNG bytes; selected CNN checkpoint','Output: six scores, material, timing and guidance','1  Reject empty, oversized, unsupported or corrupt bytes.','2  Verify dimensions; orient; convert/composite to RGB.','3  Load cached checkpoint; set evaluation mode.','4  Resize to 64 x 64; scale /255; create CHW tensor.','5  Run six logits under inference_mode; validate values.','6  Apply softmax; select argmax in canonical class order.','7  Show preview, scores, limitations and material guidance.']:b.p(line,'Code')
    # PAGE 9
    b.page();b.h('3.4  Implementation and Design Decisions')
    b.p('For a convolution, forward work is proportional to output height × output width × input channels × output channels × kernel area. Summing across four blocks and adding dense products describes CNN compute. With fixed 64 by 64 inputs, network work is bounded per image. Decoding depends on source dimensions, which is why byte and pixel limits are enforced. Weights require O(P) memory for P parameters; inference retains feature activations without an autograd training graph.')
    b.p('Table 2 connects the midterm core with the final product. CPU is supported, and CUDA is selected when available. Resource caching avoids loading weights on every click. Displayed latency covers forward plus softmax and excludes decoding, preprocessing and transfer; it is not total upload-to-result time.')
    b.table(['Decision','Alternative','Rationale'],[['Reuse aug_none','Pretrained replacement','Preserve original research and comparability.'],['Shared inference','Separate transforms','Avoid preprocessing or label mismatch.'],['Preview then Predict','Immediate prediction','Allow inspection before interpreting output.'],['Separate guidance file','Policy in model code','Maintain sources independently.'],['Pending scenarios','Invent missing scores','Do not imply field testing occurred.']],'Key implementation decisions.',sample=4)
    b.p('Guidance is stored in six JSON entries. General preparation uses official material guidance [11], while the Vietnamese source distinguishes reusable/recyclable, food and other household waste [12]. Six model labels are not six mandated bins. Local acceptance must be checked with the collector; no bin colors or campus-specific guarantees are invented. Editing guidance does not alter model scores. The interface includes warnings about hazardous items and makes clear that a trash label is not a safety classification.')
    # PAGE 10
    b.page();b.h('4  Experimental Methodology and Results',1);b.h('4.1  Experimental Setup')
    b.p('TrashNet has 2,527 images [1]. Seed 42 produces 1,766 training, 326 validation and 435 test images. Original runs use Adam [13], learning rate 0.002, weighted cross-entropy, batch size 32 and 25 epochs. ReduceLROnPlateau monitors validation loss; each run restores its minimum-validation-loss epoch. The selected aug_none is epoch 24 with dropout 0.3 and no training augmentation.')
    b.p('The original runs and saved CPU stress rerun use Python 3.12.14, PyTorch 2.14.1, Pillow 13.0.0 and recorded package versions. The submission targets Python 3.11; NumPy is selected by interpreter version to avoid a Python-3.12-only release. A separate Python 3.11.16 CPU environment on an Intel Core i7-13620H passed 38 tests, with one CUDA test skipped, and reproduced 334/435 correct predictions. Compatibility measurements are separate from historical timings; no benchmark figures are replaced.')
    b.p('scripts/run_experiments.py reproduces training, baselines and ablations when data is available. scripts/evaluate_stress_tests.py evaluates frozen manifests and records hashes, environment and per-image scores. Timing uses batch one, five warmups and CUDA synchronization if applicable. CPU times exclude decoding, resizing and device transfer and are machine-specific.')
    b.h('4.2  Evaluation Metrics')
    b.p('Let Mₐ,b count true class a predicted as b. For class k, TP is its diagonal count, FP the remaining column sum and FN the remaining row sum. Equations (8)–(10) define accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and the fixed six-class macro average. A zero denominator yields zero. Macro-F1 gives the minority trash class equal weight, while accuracy alone can hide its errors.')
    b.equation('accuracy');b.equation('pr');b.equation('f1')
    # PAGE 11
    b.page();b.h('4.3  Results')
    b.p('Table 3 compares original baselines, the midterm selected CNN and the same CNN inside the final pipeline. The final standard rerun reproduces 334/435 correct predictions. These are single-seed results; mean ± standard deviation across training runs is unavailable. The original 95% image-bootstrap Macro-F1 interval is 70.9%–79.9%, not seed uncertainty.')
    b.table(['Method','Accuracy','Macro-F1'],[['Majority baseline','23.4%','6.3%'],['Histogram centroid','38.4%','35.3%'],['Midterm selected CNN','76.8%','75.7%'],['Final pipeline, same CNN','76.8%','75.7%']],'Original test benchmark and final standard rerun. One training seed; 435 images.',sample=5,best_rows=(2,3))
    b.p('Table 4 lists validation-only ablations from saved runs. These are not test results. Depth variants also differ in channel progression; no_reg removes augmentation and dropout but retains Batch Normalization.')
    rows=[]
    for name in ['base','aug_none','aug_full','drop_0.0','drop_0.5','depth_2','depth_3','lr_2e-4','lr_1e-2','no_reg']:
        r=json.loads((root/f'results/run_{name}.json').read_text());c=r['config'];rows.append([name,f"{len(c['filters'])} / {c['dropout']}",f"{r['validation']['macro_f1']:.3f}"])
    b.table(['Configuration','Depth / dropout','Validation Macro-F1'],rows,'Existing validation ablations with the same primary seed.',sample=5)
    # PAGE 12
    b.page();b.h('4.4 Discussion')
    b.p('The CNN exceeds both simple baselines on this held-out split. This supports learning useful features for TrashNet, but not reliability on arbitrary waste photographs. The final product does not improve benchmark accuracy because it retains the checkpoint. Its measured contribution is consistent integration and additional evaluation rather than a higher score.')
    b.p('Selection is constrained. no_reg achieved validation Macro-F1 0.815, above aug_none at 0.720, but zero dropout violates the architecture requirement. We selected the best eligible dropout-positive model. This does not prove no_reg overfits or augmentation is generally harmful; one seed and a 25-epoch budget limit such conclusions.')
    b.image('confusion.png',4.85,'Standard-rerun confusion matrix. Rows are true classes and columns predictions. Source images: TrashNet [1].')
    b.p('Figure 4 shows glass→metal 16 errors, metal→glass 13 and glass→plastic 9. Original F1 is strongest for paper (0.878) and cardboard (0.864), with glass (0.670), trash (0.679) and metal (0.697) weaker. Reflection, shape and labels are plausible factors, but this experiment does not isolate their causal effects.')
    # PAGE 13
    b.page();b.h('5  Scenario Evaluation and Failure Analysis',1);b.h('5.1  Scenario Results')
    b.p('Table 5 separates the four conditions, including synthetic and real-camera variants. Labels and provenance are frozen before new inference. Standard and synthetic tests reuse held-out sources; the complex set is a visually selected 12-image subset. These are not independent deployment samples.')
    rows=[]
    for name,title in [('standard','S1 Standard'),('complex','S2 Complex subset'),('low_light_blur_synthetic','S3a Synthetic'),('low_light_blur_real','S3b Camera'),('campus_novel','S4 Campus')]:
        r=stress[name];rows.append([title,str(r['n_samples']),f"{r['accuracy']:.3f}" if r['n_samples'] else 'Pending',f"{r['macro_f1']:.3f}" if r['n_samples'] else 'Pending'])
    b.table(['Scenario','Images','Accuracy','Macro-F1'],rows,'Scenario results. Pending means unexecuted, not zero performance.',sample=6)
    b.p('Each of 435 sources yields four lossless variants outside the original directories: 1,740 transformed images but 435 source groups. Aggregate bootstrap resampling groups variants by source. Intervals are withheld for small or class-incomplete groups, including the complex subset. Filename source IDs do not prove distinct physical objects in original TrashNet.')
    settings={'dark':'Brightness ×0.30','low_contrast':'Contrast ×0.35','defocus':'Gaussian radius 2 px','motion':'Motion kernel 9 px'}
    rows=[[settings[n],f"{r['accuracy']:.3f}",f"{r['macro_f1']:.3f}"] for n,r in stress['low_light_blur_synthetic']['by_transform'].items()]
    b.table(['Transformation','Accuracy','Macro-F1'],rows,'Controlled transformations at source resolution; 435 images per row.',sample=5)
    b.p('Table 6 exposes greater degradation from darkness and reduced contrast than these blur strengths. The finding is specific to the selected parameters. Genuine camera and campus photographs remain necessary; generated variants cannot substitute for field testing.')
    # PAGE 14
    b.page();b.h('5.2  Failure Analysis')
    b.p('Figure 5 gives concrete failures: cardboard331.jpg is predicted as paper with score 99.87%, and cardboard254.jpg as paper with score 99.03%. Both are held-out images. Printed packaging is a plausible confound, but no label-removal experiment was run. The confirmed failure is wrong material classification despite high confidence; its visual cause remains unproven.')
    b.image('errors.png',5.7,'Two most confident incorrect standard predictions, read from saved per-image results. Images: TrashNet [1].')
    b.p('A 50% threshold misses these and the other highly confident errors. Calibration on separate data is future work, not a validated safety mechanism. Darkening exposes loss of useful image information across classes. Lighting advice may help a user capture a clearer object, but cannot be described as a measured accuracy gain without a controlled study. New photographs must remain unseen rather than being used to adjust the model and then reported as independent evidence.')
    b.h('5.3  Improvement over the Midterm')
    b.p('The final product adds upload validation, preview, six-score bars, retained result state, guidance and four analytical pages. Tests cover these flows, including clearing scores when an image changes. Browser checks cover desktop and 390-pixel layouts. The evaluation framework adds 1,740 perturbations and reveals a decrease from 0.757 to 0.484 aggregate Macro-F1. Standard accuracy changes by zero because the core is unchanged. Independent campus and real-camera evaluations are still incomplete and visibly pending.')
    # PAGE 15
    b.page();b.h('6  Limitations Ethics and Conclusion',1);b.h('6.1  Limitations and Future Work')
    b.p('The evidence is limited by one seed, 25 training epochs and possible physical-object leakage across an image-level split. Object identities are unavailable, so filename IDs cannot establish independent objects. Resizing to 64 by 64 loses detail. The taxonomy forces unsupported inputs into known labels, and softmax can be highly confident on mistakes. The complex subset and controlled transforms do not estimate campus deployment accuracy.')
    b.p('Priorities are an independently labeled, distinct-object campus challenge set; real low-light and blur captures; and multiple training seeds. Any calibration or threshold choice must use separate validation data and be frozen before the challenge test. These proposals are not completed experiments. More crushed, occluded and cluttered items are needed to cover requested difficult conditions.')
    b.h('6.2  Ethical Considerations')
    b.p('The principal risks are over-trust, unsuitable disposal instructions and exposure of private images. The product identifies itself as an educational prototype, explains unknown-class limits and provides cautions. It does not determine hazardous-waste safety or guarantee acceptance. Campus photos should exclude identifiable people and private information. Labels should be independent of predictions; Macro-F1 and per-class scores prevent the weakest classes being hidden by accuracy.')
    b.p('Core inference is local without hosted AI APIs. Uploaded images are processed for a session rather than deliberately added to training. A local server still needs appropriate host access controls. Guidance cites authoritative sources and requires users to confirm local arrangements. Appendix C discloses generative AI assistance; the team remains responsible for implementation, evidence and references.')
    b.h('6.3  Conclusion')
    b.p('We integrated the original six-class CNN into a functional product and reproduced 76.8% held-out accuracy and 75.7% Macro-F1. Final-stage testing exposed marked sensitivity to darkness and low contrast, while the interface improved transparency and usability. A working product must communicate limitations as clearly as predictions. Independent campus and camera evidence remains necessary before broader reliability claims are justified.')
    # FRONT MATTER, REFERENCES AND APPENDICES ARE OUTSIDE THE BODY COUNT
    b.page();b.h('References',1)
    refs=[
      'G. Thung and M. Yang, “TrashNet: Dataset of images of trash,” 2016. https://github.com/garythung/trashnet (accessed Nov. 19, 2026).',
      'A. Paszke et al., “PyTorch: An Imperative Style, High-Performance Deep Learning Library,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, vol. 32, 2019. https://papers.nips.cc/paper/9015-pytorch-an-imperative-style-high-performance-deep-learning-library.',
      'PyTorch contributors, “Conv2d,” PyTorch documentation. https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.Conv2d.html (accessed Nov. 19, 2026).',
      'S. Ioffe and C. Szegedy, “Batch Normalization: Accelerating Deep Network Training by Reducing Internal Covariate Shift,” arXiv:1502.03167, 2015. https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03167.',
      'N. Srivastava et al., “Dropout: A Simple Way to Prevent Neural Networks from Overfitting,” Journal of Machine Learning Research, vol. 15, no. 56, pp. 1929–1958, 2014. https://jmlr.org/papers/v15/srivastava14a.html.',
      'C. Guo, G. Pleiss, Y. Sun, and K. Q. Weinberger, “On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks,” in Proc. 34th International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 70, pp. 1321–1330, 2017. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/guo17a.html.',
      'Streamlit, “Streamlit documentation.” https://docs.streamlit.io/ (accessed Nov. 19, 2026).',
      'Pillow contributors, “Pillow 13.0.0 documentation.” https://pillow.readthedocs.io/en/stable/ (accessed Nov. 19, 2026).',
      'C. R. Harris et al., “Array programming with NumPy,” Nature, vol. 585, pp. 357–362, 2020, doi: 10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2.',
      'J. D. Hunter, “Matplotlib: A 2D Graphics Environment,” Computing in Science & Engineering, vol. 9, no. 3, pp. 90–95, 2007, doi: 10.1109/MCSE.2007.55.',
      'U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, “How Do I Recycle Common Recyclables.” https://www.epa.gov/recycle/how-do-i-recycle-common-recyclables (accessed Nov. 19, 2026).',
      'Vietnam Environment Agency, “Source separation of waste [Phan loai rac thai tai nguon],” Apr. 5, 2024. https://vea.mae.gov.vn/tin-tuc-su-kien/10526/phan-loai-rac-thai-tai-nguo (accessed Nov. 19, 2026).',
      'D. P. Kingma and J. Ba, “Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization,” in Proc. International Conference on Learning Representations, 2015, arXiv:1412.6980. https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980.']
    for i,text in enumerate(refs,1):b.p(f'[{i}]\t{text}','Reference')
    b.page();b.h('Appendix A  Reproducibility',1)
    b.p('Repository: https://github.com/IHateCoding0502/project-11-waste-classifier. The code directory retains the starter layout. Python 3.11 is the submission target. Install dependencies before offline operation. The application performs local CNN inference without network APIs; its checkpoint is generated by the project training code, not pretrained solution weights.')
    for line in ['py -3.11 -m venv .venv','.\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install --upgrade pip','.\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.txt','.\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m streamlit run app.py']:b.p(line,'Code')
    b.p('For tests, install requirements-dev.txt and run the following. Included synthetic images test software behavior only and are not held-out performance evidence.')
    for line in ['.\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1','python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt','python -m pytest tests -q','python scripts/validate_checkpoint.py','python scripts/collect_dataset_info.py']:b.p(line,'Code')
    b.p('For full-data reproduction, obtain the pinned TrashNet archive as documented in data/LICENSE.md, prepare the fixed splits and run the original experiments. The ZIP intentionally excludes data/train, data/validation and data/test. Acquisition is preparation, not an inference dependency. Training and evaluation work offline once the images are available.')
    for line in ['python scripts/prepare_dataset.py --confirm','python scripts/run_experiments.py --epochs 25 --seed 42','python scripts/build_analysis_figures.py','python scripts/evaluate_stress_tests.py --prepare --device cpu']:b.p(line,'Code')
    b.p('Tables 1–2 document the implemented specification and design. Table 3 reads results/baselines.json, selected.json and the standard rerun; Table 4 reads run_*.json. Tables 5–6 read results/stress/metrics.json. Figure 4 is generated by the stress evaluator; Figure 5 reads predictions.json and source images. Figures 1–2 are actual UI screenshots. Figure 3 is drawn by build_submission_report.py. Rebuilding figures requires the source files; all numeric results are retained in machine-readable form.')
    b.p('Selected checkpoint SHA-256')
    b.p('825083ce60c04c0566b736a2089ece09f9a640ba0b72b55ce3b1fadf6ef077c3','Code')
    b.p('Evaluations record checkpoint, manifest and image hashes. Retraining on different hardware need not reproduce scores bit-for-bit; do not edit new results to force agreement. The report generator and Word field-refresh helper support later editing.')
    b.page();b.h('Appendix B  Member Contributions',1)
    b.p('The representative is marked with an asterisk. Task descriptions and integer percentages must reflect the agreement of all group members.')
    rows=[]
    for m in b.team['members']:rows.append([m['student_id'],m['name']+('*' if m['representative'] else ''),m['tasks'] or 'Awaiting group confirmation',str(m['percent']) if m['percent'] is not None else 'Pending'])
    b.table(['Student ID','Full name','Main tasks','%'],rows,'Contribution of each member.',sample=7)
    if all(isinstance(m['percent'],int) for m in b.team['members']):b.p(f"Total: {sum(m['percent'] for m in b.team['members'])}%. Group agreement: {'confirmed' if b.team['contributions_agreed'] else 'not yet confirmed'}.")
    representative=next(m for m in b.team['members'] if m['representative'])
    b.p(f"Representative and submitter: {representative['name']}, student ID {representative['student_id']}.")
    b.h('Appendix C  Use of Generative AI Tools',1)
    b.p('Generative AI assistance was used on this repository and report. Generated text was not substituted for measured results. The team remains responsible for checking the implementation, citations, numbers and submission requirements.')
    b.table(['Tool','Purpose','Affected work'],[['OpenAI Codex','Implementation, debugging, UI redesign, test assistance and report drafting.','Streamlit, upload handling, evaluation tools, documentation and report.'],['Qwen2.5-VL 3B via Ollama','Historical exploratory 30-image comparison; not the final core.','Saved comparison CSV files and Section 2.3 discussion.']],'Disclosed generative AI assistance.',sample=8)
